{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1389767-what-is-supposed-to-be-the-benefit-of-imbue-item","id":1389767,"title":"What is supposed to be the benefit of Imbue Item?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-05-18T15:22:20-07:00","id":1389767,"title":"What is supposed to be the benefit of Imbue Item?","content":"Is there something besides the Spell Control reduction and having a mage with no dots in an arcana cast a spell from it?<br /> I dont feel the benefit of Imbuing an item for personal use.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1351-sakii","name":"Sakii","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1351_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-18T15:41:15-07:00","id":1389771,"title":"","content":"If you can Imbue an item you can cast <i>Word of Command</i>, allowing you to max out the spell factors without actually affecting your dice pool.<br /> <br /> You can make the Imbued item trigger something that would happen as part of another action: e.g. imbue a weapon so that it casts a deleterious spell on whatever it strikes. Yes, this costs mana and won't always work (you still have to roll for the spell) but it is the only way to hit someone with a weapon and blast the with magic.<br /> <br /> The spell is more difficult to trace to you, even if you're the one that made the item.<br /> <br /> The &quot;always on&quot; mode can be useful for defensive effects.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1452235-proin-s-legacy-hub\" target=\"_blank\">proin's Legacy hub</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2757-proindrakenzol","name":"proindrakenzol","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1514"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-19T08:35:33-07:00","id":1389983,"title":"","content":"Imbued items can also be used by sleepwalker allies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","name":"galivet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13121_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"283"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-19T08:40:48-07:00","id":1389984,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>proindrakenzol</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1389767-what-is-supposed-to-be-the-benefit-of-imbue-item?p=1389771#post1389771\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you can Imbue an item you can cast <i>Word of Command</i>, allowing you to max out the spell factors without actually affecting your dice pool.<br /> <br /> You can make the Imbued item trigger something that would happen as part of another action: e.g. imbue a weapon so that it casts a deleterious spell on whatever it strikes. Yes, this costs mana and won't always work (you still have to roll for the spell) but it is the only way to hit someone with a weapon and blast the with magic.<br /> <br /> The spell is more difficult to trace to you, even if you're the one that made the item.<br /> <br /> The &quot;always on&quot; mode can be useful for defensive effects.</div> </div> </div> </div>So I wonder if we can get some clarification on this from <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2229-meghan-fitzgerald\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2229_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2229\" data-vbnamecard=\"2229\">Meghan Fitzgerald</a> (sorry but I see you answer a lot of things in Changeling etc). Is it really the intention that one can bypass the action economy by having multiple imbued items all triggering from one condition/thing and having those multiple spells going off at once. A mage being able to wear 10 rings of fireball triggered by &quot;wearer saying blamo&quot; then sets off all those 10 rings and is able to have 10 fireball spells in one round cast at whatever?<br /> <br /> My understanding has always been that using an imbued item takes your action as you need to spend time directing the item as it casts the spell and that triggers just give you access to the items spell and don't actually start the casting process itself. Are imbued items with triggers able to cast without a user via triggers? Yes we had examples of these sort of items in 1e but everything I've read in 2e always has a user being implied somewhere. My question is always how does a imbued item with a trigger detect when its trigger occurs if an item can cast its spell without a user and just via a trigger.<br /> <br /> I mean I've also heard the argument that imbued items cast their spell reflexively and don't require an action or ritual time like every other spell simply because they have a trigger and one can say &quot;blame&quot; more than once in 3 seconds. Which also seems silly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1534,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","user":"totalgit","date":". 05-20-2020, 12:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-19T15:11:57-07:00","id":1390074,"title":"","content":"You can very easily break the game through having imbued items go over and beyond the spell limit. They can also be used as large stores of free mana, which isn't usually a huge concern but can be used in a pinch.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25423-voytek","name":"Voytek","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25423_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"63"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T15:54:54-07:00","id":1391798,"title":"","content":"A spell in an Imbued Item takes its usual casting time according to the factors the creator used when they made it, whether the spell is Contingent or not. The only way you can cast a spell reflexively through an Imbued Item is if the creator of the item used Reach to imbue one of the handful of spells that can be made reflexive via Reach (like Exceptional Luck or Suppress Life). A trigger still requires a user to trigger it, and the user must direct the item to cast, meaning they must take an action in accordance with the spell's casting time as though they were themselves casting the spell.<br /> <br /> The benefits of an Imbued Item are: anyone can use it, even a Sleeper; it can act as a Mana battery, allowing you to use the item's Mana instead of your own to cast the spell or (if you're a mage) spend it to mitigate Paradox; with an exceptional success on the casting roll, you can relinquish the spell without a Willpower point/dot; if you use an Imbued Item that you didn't create, the spell you cast with it doesn't count toward your own spell control limit; casting with an Imbued Item means you don't leave your Nimbus around for others to find; and you can use Imbued Items to cast spells you wouldn't otherwise have the Arcana dots to cast, or cast them sympathetically even if you don't actually have the Attainments to do that, if the creator made them that way. Also, since the spell remains under the creator's control and when it's cast they can alter the spell factors and such, you could make one, give it to someone else, have them activate the spell while you're elsewhere, but still control how it's cast (provided you're in touch with that person or scrying on them or whatever so you know what you're doing). And if your item falls into the wrong hands, you can just cancel the spell or destroy the imbuement with Mana, whether you're present or not.<br /> <br /> There's an expanded explanation of Imbued Items on p. 67 of Signs of Sorcery, if you want more detail.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Meghan Fitzgerald | Onyx Path freelance writer &amp; developer</i><br /> <span style=\"color:#008000\"><i>Changeling: The Lost developer</i></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#009999\"><i>Mage: The Awakening developer</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2229-meghan-fitzgerald","name":"Meghan Fitzgerald","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2229_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"124"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T17:56:42-07:00","id":1391817,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Meghan Fitzgerald</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1389767-what-is-supposed-to-be-the-benefit-of-imbue-item?p=1391798#post1391798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A spell in an Imbued Item takes its usual casting time according to the factors the creator used when they made it, whether the spell is Contingent or not. The only way you can cast a spell reflexively through an Imbued Item is if the creator of the item used Reach to imbue one of the handful of spells that can be made reflexive via Reach (like Exceptional Luck or Suppress Life). A trigger still requires a user to trigger it, and the user must direct the item to cast, meaning they must take an action in accordance with the spell's casting time as though they were themselves casting the spell.<br /> <br /> The benefits of an Imbued Item are: anyone can use it, even a Sleeper; it can act as a Mana battery, allowing you to use the item's Mana instead of your own to cast the spell or (if you're a mage) spend it to mitigate Paradox; with an exceptional success on the casting roll, you can relinquish the spell without a Willpower point/dot; if you use an Imbued Item that you didn't create, the spell you cast with it doesn't count toward your own spell control limit; casting with an Imbued Item means you don't leave your Nimbus around for others to find; and you can use Imbued Items to cast spells you wouldn't otherwise have the Arcana dots to cast, or cast them sympathetically even if you don't actually have the Attainments to do that, if the creator made them that way. Also, since the spell remains under the creator's control and when it's cast they can alter the spell factors and such, you could make one, give it to someone else, have them activate the spell while you're elsewhere, but still control how it's cast (provided you're in touch with that person or scrying on them or whatever so you know what you're doing). And if your item falls into the wrong hands, you can just cancel the spell or destroy the imbuement with Mana, whether you're present or not.<br /> <br /> There's an expanded explanation of Imbued Items on p. 67 of Signs of Sorcery, if you want more detail.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you for your answer.<br /> The items you describe indecate the bemefits for other to use your imbued items, but could it be that only benefit of making an item for oneself is the mana battery and the ability to cast multiple spells with one control slot?<br /> <br /> About this part:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Meghan Fitzgerald</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1389767-what-is-supposed-to-be-the-benefit-of-imbue-item?p=1391798#post1391798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A spell in an Imbued Item takes its usual casting time according to the factors the creator used when they made it, whether the spell is Contingent or not.</div> </div> </div> </div>Signs of sorcery say:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SoS</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Spell Factors: The spell factors of an Imbued Item are decided by the user upon activation</div> </div> </div> </div>So arent the factors decided by the user of the item?<br /> Or do i gain the advantage of risking for high level factors in a safe place(potency 7 Celestial Strike), and then easily use the item on the field?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1351-sakii","name":"Sakii","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1351_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T20:05:13-07:00","id":1391844,"title":"","content":"So for a imbued item gun that has a fireball spell triggered to cast when the item is fired, A user would need to fire the gun on his first action, and then on his next action he can activate the imbued item, which takes his entire action? Or could the user fire the gun without rolling (effectively shooting the floor or whatever) and activate the imbued item on the first round? I personally would allow this, since the user isn't shooting somebody AND casting a spell on them at the same time.<br /> <br /> How long after the triggering condition can a user wait to cast a spell from an imbued item? I assume it has to be cast on the next action for it to count? You couldn't fire the gun and then wait 2 hours to activate the item, is there a set time frame?<br /> <br /> This also stops people from accidentally triggering an imbued item even doing the triggering action right? As the user needs to know the trigger to start and direct the item as it casts its spell. Can a user try random things that might include the triggering condition and then get the imbued item to cast its spell bypassing the need to know its trigger?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1534,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","user":"totalgit","date":". 05-27-2020, 02:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T20:47:48-07:00","id":1391850,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sakii</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1389767-what-is-supposed-to-be-the-benefit-of-imbue-item?p=1391817#post1391817\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So arent the factors decided by the user of the item?<br /> Or do i gain the advantage of risking for high level factors in a safe place(potency 7 Celestial Strike), and then easily use the item on the field?</div> </div> </div> </div>The spell factors are decided on cast, but the reach options (including Advanced Spell Factors) are decided on creation, based on SoS.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2229-meghan-fitzgerald\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2229_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2229\" data-vbnamecard=\"2229\">Meghan Fitzgerald</a>: If the item was created with Instant Casting, and the trigger is &quot;whack someone,&quot; wouldn't that work since the action (hitting someone with the item) is an instant action, just like the cast time requirement?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1452235-proin-s-legacy-hub\" target=\"_blank\">proin's Legacy hub</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2757-proindrakenzol","name":"proindrakenzol","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1514"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T22:09:59-07:00","id":1391860,"title":"","content":"It's the item that casts the spell (using the character's Gnosis, if any), not the character. The item begins casting the spell immediately after its trigger condition occurs to completion. It doesn't cast its spell simultaneously with the occurrence of its trigger condition unless the spell itself is reflexive via an established Reach option. It can't delay casting its spell after it's triggered (unless I suppose Flexible Reach allows the creator of the spell to shift from Instant to Ritual casting time).<br /> <br /> A main benefit of imbued items is enjoying persistent spell effects of Indefinite duration that didn't require a Willpower dot to relinquish (exception success) and don't consume a spell control slot. Imagine a Ring of Wards and Signs -- whoever wears it gets the benefit of that protection forever unless it's dispelled, and even then it can be reactivated by a Prime 1 spell. It's really good.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13121,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","user":"galivet","date":". 05-27-2020, 01:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","name":"galivet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13121_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"283"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T22:59:11-07:00","id":1391867,"title":"","content":"Similarly, there have always been items that have triggered themselves due to conditions in the area. If it can only be activated by a direct action in 2e that actually has a effect on the setting of my games, where things like Imbued booby-traps aren't exactly common, but do exist.<br /> <br /> This means you couldn't, say, cast &quot;Imbue Room&quot; (SoS) and then Imbue a Forces spell in a &quot;No Smoking&quot; area to snuff out illicit cigarettes with &quot;Lights Up&quot; as the trigger. The person who lit the cigarette would have to intentionally put it out themselves.<br /> <br /> Examples from various books of Imbued Items &amp; Artifacts that do trigger based on conditions: <a href=\"https://imgur.com/a/dwflEO7\" target=\"_blank\">https://imgur.com/a/dwflEO7</a><br /> * Mana Bowl: Triggers when a Hallow generates mana<br /> * Supernal Server: Triggers when someone without authorization tries to access it<br /> * Sentinels' Shield: Triggers when spells are cast on the wearer<br /> * Shackles of the Sleeper: Triggers when a lone mage sees them","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11818,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11818-romzombie","user":"ROMzombie","date":". 05-27-2020, 02:23 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11818-romzombie","name":"ROMzombie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11818_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"35"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T23:17:52-07:00","id":1391869,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure what you mean; SoS explicitly calls out &quot;condition&quot; as a potential trigger for a contingent imbued item. The item will begin casting its spell once the trigger condition has finished occurring.<br /> <br /> Maybe you guys can get whatever you want by being more creative with how you define trigger conditions? Defining the trigger as &quot;a guy steps on this part of the floor and then fifteen second pass&quot; is fine; it just has to be set that way as part of imbuing the item.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13121,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","user":"galivet","date":". 05-27-2020, 02:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","name":"galivet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13121_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"283"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-26T23:37:44-07:00","id":1391871,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>galivet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1389767-what-is-supposed-to-be-the-benefit-of-imbue-item?p=1391860#post1391860\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's the item that casts the spell (using the character's Gnosis, if any), not the character. The item begins casting the spell immediately after its trigger condition occurs to completion. It doesn't cast its spell simultaneously with the occurrence of its trigger condition unless the spell itself is reflexive via an established Reach option. It can't delay casting its spell after it's triggered (unless I suppose Flexible Reach allows the creator of the spell to shift from Instant to Ritual casting time).<br /> <br /> A main benefit of imbued items is enjoying persistent spell effects of Indefinite duration that didn't require a Willpower dot to relinquish (exception success) and don't consume a spell control slot. Imagine a Ring of Wards and Signs -- whoever wears it gets the benefit of that protection forever unless it's dispelled, and even then it can be reactivated by a Prime 1 spell. It's really good.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think the item starts casting when its trigger goes off until a user directs it too (triggers just let you activate the item), that user then has to direct it for the entire casting time the item takes. A drum item that lets you cast a spell when its hit won't cast a spell every time it gets hit just because its trigger is getting completed. It'll only cast once the user directs it too after being hit. I see it like a safety on a gun, you turn the safety off (do the triggering action) to let yourself shoot the gun, the gun doesn't go off just by switching the safety. Hence my question above, if the items trigger is banging on it, but a person doesn't know this, can they activate the item just by banging on the drum or do they need to know that banging on the drum will let the item cast the spell first?<br /> <br /> Also aren't all the examples Rom has posted from 1e?? and not relevant for 2e. Sentinals shield already breaks 2e's rule of only 1 contingent spell per item anyways. (and doesn't even mention what the shields trigger is, it could just be touching the shield like a startrek combadge which then lets the user cast either spell)<br /> <br /> The room example would mean that anybody who knows the trigger and is inside the room(ie lightning up) could have the room cast the forces spell to snuff it out. It doesn't mean that every time somebody &quot;lights up&quot; the forces spell is automatically triggered on its own and snuffs it out. For the room to do what you want you could make it a persistent effect force spell that would douse all fires up to whatever potency that enter the room.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1534,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","user":"totalgit","date":". 05-27-2020, 04:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-27T07:40:04-07:00","id":1391927,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1389767-what-is-supposed-to-be-the-benefit-of-imbue-item?p=1391871#post1391871\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't think the item starts casting when its trigger goes off until a user directs it too (triggers just let you activate the item), that user then has to direct it for the entire casting time the item takes. A drum item that lets you cast a spell when its hit won't cast a spell every time it gets hit just because its trigger is getting completed. It'll only cast once the user directs it too after being hit. I see it like a safety on a gun, you turn the safety off (do the triggering action) to let yourself shoot the gun, the gun doesn't go off just by switching the safety.</div> </div> </div> </div>What you describe is reasonable if that's how you want to play but I don't think it's the most straightforward reading of the rules. Some snippets from core and SoS:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> contingent (triggered by a word, gesture, or condition) </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Contingent Items are triggered by a word, gesture, or condition </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Any character that knows the trigger method may activate an Imbued Item, causing it to cast. </div> </div> </div>\u200b<br /> I think the third quote there is the point of ambiguity. Does it mean, &quot;Any character that knows the trigger method may [satisfy it to] activate an Imbued Item, causing it to cast.&quot; or does it mean &quot;Any character that knows the trigger method may [satisfy it and then] activate an Imbued Item, causing it to cast.&quot;<br /> <br /> For me, the use of the word &quot;trigger&quot; in the first two quotes implies that the satisfying the contingent condition/word/gesture/whatever is the trigger of the &quot;gun&quot; rather than its safety. Imagine a Sleepwalker using an imbued item. She satisfies the contingent condition and then has to do... something else to make the item cast? What would a Sleepwalker even be able to do in this arena beyond satisfying the trigger condition? Focus hard? Make a wish? They're magically impotent.<br /> <br /> EDIT: To clarify, I see the user's &quot;direction&quot; of the imbued item's casting, like the contribution of her Gnosis to the dice pool, as involuntary. Once user triggers the item, she has no choice but to immediately decide spell factors and select a target if appropriate. I would rather have had all the details of casting, including the dice pool, pinned down as part of the creation of the item. Like I would want a Stick of Shooting Lightning to function exactly the same way upon every activation, regardless of who is activating it, and have the subject of the spell determined by an Aimed Spell roll. That's just how the Magical Item trope works in my mind.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13121,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","user":"galivet","date":". 05-27-2020, 10:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","name":"galivet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13121_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"283"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-27T09:14:55-07:00","id":1391962,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>galivet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1389767-what-is-supposed-to-be-the-benefit-of-imbue-item?p=1391927#post1391927\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What you describe is reasonable if that's how you want to play but I don't think it's the most straightforward reading of the rules. Some snippets from core and SoS:<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> \u200b<br /> I think the third quote there is the point of ambiguity. Does it mean, &quot;Any character that knows the trigger method may [satisfy it to] activate an Imbued Item, causing it to cast.&quot; or does it mean &quot;Any character that knows the trigger method may [satisfy it and then] activate an Imbued Item, causing it to cast.&quot;<br /> <br /> For me, the use of the word &quot;trigger&quot; in the first two quotes implies that the satisfying the contingent condition/word/gesture/whatever is the trigger of the &quot;gun&quot; rather than its safety. Imagine a Sleepwalker using an imbued item. She satisfies the contingent condition and then has to do... something else to make the item cast? What would a Sleepwalker even be able to do in this arena beyond satisfying the trigger condition? Focus hard? Make a wish? They're magically impotent.<br /> <br /> EDIT: To clarify, I see the user's &quot;direction&quot; of the imbued item's casting, like the contribution of her Gnosis to the dice pool, as involuntary. Once user triggers the item, she has no choice but to immediately decide spell factors and select a target if appropriate. I would rather have had all the details of casting, including the dice pool, pinned down as part of the creation of the item. Like I would want a Stick of Shooting Lightning to function exactly the same way upon every activation, regardless of who is activating it, and have the subject of the spell determined by an Aimed Spell roll. That's just how the Magical Item trope works in my mind.</div> </div> </div> </div>The issue I have with a trigger just casting the spell is what if the person can't or doesn't want to spend time directing the spell, what happens then. The user might not be able to spend ritual time directing the spell if its reach wasn't used to make it instant for example. Does the spell then just fizzle as it's not directed or does it go off and hit random people with random spell factors? The third quote to me seems like two parts (Character that knows the trigger method) may (activate the item, causing it to cast).<br /> <br /> The word &quot;may&quot; also points to a choice, the character may or may not activate the item, it's not worded as (Character that knows trigger method) must (activate the item, causing it to cast).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-27T14:44:39-07:00","id":1392054,"title":"","content":"I don't think there's a way to handle it consistently that satisfies all the desires people have for imbued items.<br /> <br /> I want Atlantean ruins with imbued item booby traps that cast harmful spells when intruders draw near. In that case, the &quot;user&quot; is the person who sprang the trap and the trigger condition is getting too close. Giving the trap's would-be victim the option to just not &quot;use&quot; the imbued item after triggering it defeats the point. This kind of thing is all over the fiction; it's clearly meant to be part of the game universe.<br /> <br /> I like the idea that when someone triggers an imbued item -- intentionally or not -- they're immediately locked in to whatever the casting entails. You see this kind of thing in popular fiction all the time, and it's dramatic -- clueless sidekick touches the forbidden mcguffin and suddenly &quot;WAAAAHHHH I CAN'T STOP IT!!!&quot; and horrible consequences ensue. The sidekick is pinned in place, hands glued to the mcguffin, utterly captive to the spell it's casting until it's done. That's classic Mage-style horror right there.<br /> <br /> I have no reference point in my imagination for someone triggering a magical item and then just deciding that nothing happens as a result. I don't understand how that happening would fit into a story. Like there is no point where Harry Potter puts his magic cloak over his head but then decides he didn't want to be invisible after all; he was just cold. Dumbledore didn't get to drink the Emerald Potion and then just decide not to follow through on experiencing the despair and thirst that drinking it triggered. From a dramatic standpoint, if someone didn't want to activate the item, then they shouldn't have satisfied the trigger condition. And if they didn't know the trigger condition and activated it by accident, <i>it's interesting and fun when something they didn't expect results.</i><br /> <br /> I'm just not clear on what scenario you want to make happen in gameplay that needs imbued items to work as you're describing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13121,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","user":"galivet","date":". 05-27-2020, 06:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","name":"galivet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13121_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"283"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-27T14:51:33-07:00","id":1392057,"title":"","content":"Also the wording in that third quote is just really sloppy. RAW, merely knowing the trigger enables a user to activate an imbued item. Like if the trigger is &quot;bathe it in werewolf blood,&quot; a user can activate the item merely by knowing that fact rather than by actually bathing the item in werewolf blood. So I don't really care to parse that snippet too closely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","name":"galivet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13121_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"283"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-27T18:00:51-07:00","id":1392084,"title":"","content":"I would say it could be done with a spell. Prime 2 for a mix of Remote Control and Words of Command. Then mix in Fate 1 for Oaths Fullfilled to detect the condition. Then relinquish that and you have a magical trap.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>New experiences are the font of creativity, when seeking inspiration, break your routine.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1278855-the-agathos-kai-sophos-a-acanthus-legacy-of-strategists\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Agathos Kai Sophos</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Acanthus Legacy of strategists (Mind/Time)</span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1360568-the-szary-stra%C5%BCnik-an-obrimos-legacy-of-scholars-of-the-glyphs-of-fate\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Szary Stra\u017cnik</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Obrimos Legacy whose invisible hands guide through the Glyphs of Fate (Fate/Prime)</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","name":"KaiserAfini","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24280_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2648"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-28T00:04:53-07:00","id":1392135,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>galivet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1389767-what-is-supposed-to-be-the-benefit-of-imbue-item?p=1392054#post1392054\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think there's a way to handle it consistently that satisfies all the desires people have for imbued items.<br /> <br /> I'm just not clear on what scenario you want to make happen in gameplay that needs imbued items to work as you're describing.</div> </div> </div> </div>I found it very odd that people think/thought that imbued items could allow multiple spells to be cast in one action just because its trigger happens multiple times in a turn that's all. (And be unique across the entire Chronicles of Darkness since even Time 5 doesn't allow for multiple actions etc.) Needing to do the trigger and then wait for an action is only a scenario for me if a user is trying to complete two actual actions at the same time like rolling dex + firearms to shoot somebody and using an imbued item casting a spell because its trigger is a gun fired. I would totally allow the user to fire the gun in the same turn and allow them to use the imbued item but you aren't going to get the dex+firearms roll on top of it in the same way id allow them to wave a wand about in the air and say &quot;Blamo&quot; at the same time as activating the imbued item. Proindrakenzol brings up an imbued sword with a &quot;strikes something&quot; trigger as an example which to me either breaks the action economy by allowing a character to get both a melee hit and a spell off in one round OR at least to me means that the character has to strike with the sword and then he can activate the item directly after it. (next turn). This is why I'm lead to believe that triggers don't start the casting process automagically since it avoids this problem.<br /> <br /> The fact you can get multiple spells going off at once by placing two or more of the same spells in &quot;Hung spell&quot; spell is a thing but if I remember correctly it was ruled or common consensus that says that multiple spells released via a hung spell being released don't stack. So 3 potency 7 bashing telekinetic strikes placed into a &quot;hung spell&quot; spell won't put an average mortal to 7 agg. (Maybe that's just my interpretation though)<br /> <br /> Items that can cast without a user are great I can obviously think of many uses for them mainly as traps which if they could be set to have a trigger as &quot;Anything enters this room&quot; would make them superior alarm systems but as i said earlier also how would they detect anything in the first place and they existed in 1e for sure, but as I said previously 2e's text always mentions having a user. Items that can cast themselves sound like an imperial practice or talisman/artifact kinda thing.<br /> <br /> I totally agree with Kaiserafini that sprinkling some fate into the item as a persistent effect esp since &quot;oaths fulfilled&quot; especially says &quot;it must be something the mage could perceive himself&quot; might work if and if I wanted to have items that could cast themselves without a user this would be how I'd do it. It would need to be a combined &quot;words of power&quot; + &quot;another spell&quot; along with a persistent fate spell as only 1 contingent spell is allowed per item, however.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1534,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","user":"totalgit","date":". 05-28-2020, 05:58 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-28T08:41:27-07:00","id":1392209,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1389767-what-is-supposed-to-be-the-benefit-of-imbue-item?p=1392135#post1392135\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Items that can cast without a user are great I can obviously think of many uses for them mainly as traps which if they could be set to have a trigger as &quot;Anything enters this room&quot; would make them superior alarm systems but as i said earlier also how would they detect anything in the first place and they existed in 1e for sure, but as I said previously 2e's text always mentions having a user. Items that can cast themselves sound like an imperial practice or talisman/artifact kinda thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah I don't think the rules clarify this sufficiently.<br /> <br /> SoS states &quot;Imbued Items cast their spell as though they were mages, directed by the user.&quot; The user satisfies the trigger, and then the item itself uses its action to cast the spell as a consequence. But what about the case where the trigger gets satisfied but there's no user? For example, trigger condition &quot;whenever it gets submerged in water.&quot; The character who has the item has a heart attack and drops it into a tide pool as he dies. No one is around when the water fills the pool, so there's no user. Does it activate? I just wouldn't let this come up in a game because the rules don't address it.<br /> <br /> For the case of magical traps, alarm systems, etc... I rule that the user is the victim of those systems. The rules don't state conclusively that the user of an imbued item must use it willingly. My view is that the user <i>must</i> direct the casting, not that they <i>may</i> direct the casting. It's involuntary. The awkwardness for me is that the user has any agency over the casting at all; I just don't like that aspect of imbued items. Like when an item is activated by a sleepwalker, that guy knows fuck-all about spell factors and shouldn't be able to direct the casting of anything. For me, it's the <i>player</i> who directs the casting rather than the character; from the in-game perspective of a character the item is doing everything.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13121,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","user":"galivet","date":". 05-28-2020, 12:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","name":"galivet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13121_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"283"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}